Wallpaper Engine polls GetCursorPos on the whole desktop every frame (GH 0x140141520 → ScreenToClient
of the wallpaper's window / its size, written to ctx+0x8c), so its pointer moves continuously,
over other monitors and over windows too. A Wayland host only sees the pointer over its own
surface: when it enters the output, or comes back from behind a window, the next position is a
jump from wherever it left. g_PointerPositionLast then held the old position, and cursorripple
drew a saturated stroke along the whole jump — 3184155462's water lashed out at the wallpaper's
sides as the pointer came on.

wp_project_pointer_jump marks the next position as a jump: the frame that reads it takes it as
g_PointerPositionLast too, and a simulation's next step (0105) starts where the pointer landed.
A move after it counts as before.

